+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Virtio-msg Linux Bridge
+=======================
+
+This document describes how to use the Linux bridge backend for
+``virtio-msg`` from the QEMU command line.
+
+Overview
+--------
+
+The Linux bridge backend connects a QEMU ``virtio-msg`` transport to a host
+bridge endpoint (typically through ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``).
+
+Most users should instantiate the transport container device:
+
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport``
+
+This container model creates:
+
+- a top-level ``virtio-msg-outer-bus``
+- a ``virtio-msg`` transport
+- an internal ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` backend
+
+A standalone backend model is also available for integrators that already
+instantiate ``virtio-msg`` separately:
+
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge``
+
+Requirements
+------------
+
+- QEMU built with ``CONFIG_VIRTIO_MSG_LINUX_BRIDGE``.
+- Host bridge device node available (default: ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``).
+- A valid bridge endpoint.
+
+Endpoint Syntax
+---------------
+
+The ``endpoint`` property format is ``bus-name[:bus-id]``:
+
+- ``bus-name`` is required.
+- ``bus-id`` is optional.
+- ``bus-id`` is passed as a string to the kernel bridge backend.
+
+Examples:
+
+- ``loopback``
+- ``ffa:5``
+- ``ffa:0x5``
+
+Canonical Transport Mode
+------------------------
+
+Use ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport`` as the default entry point:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -device virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport,id=vmsgt0,endpoint=loopback \
+   -device virtio-rng-device,bus=/vmsgt0/bus/virtio-msg/bus0/virtio-msg-dev
+
+To use a non-default bridge node:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -device virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport,id=vmsgt0,endpoint=ffa:5,devnode=/dev/virtio-msg-bridge0
+
+Standalone Backend Mode
+-----------------------
+
+Standalone mode is intended for integration with an already existing
+``virtio-msg`` object:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -device virtio-msg,id=vmsg0 \
+   -device virtio-msg-linux-bridge,id=vmsgbr0,endpoint=loopback,bus=/vmsg0/msg-bus \
+   -device virtio-rng-device,bus=/vmsg0/bus0/virtio-msg-dev
+
+Use standalone mode only when you need explicit control over how
+``virtio-msg`` is instantiated.
+
+Properties
+----------
+
+Both models expose the same backend configuration properties:
+
+- ``endpoint`` (required)
+- ``create-endpoint`` (optional, default ``off``)
+- ``devnode`` (optional, default ``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``)
+- ``tx-ring-entries`` (default ``256``)
+- ``tx-ring-entry-size`` (default ``128`` in transport mode)
+- ``rx-ring-entries`` (default ``256``)
+- ``rx-ring-entry-size`` (default ``128`` in transport mode)
+
+The standalone backend also exposes:
+
+- ``vmm-max-msg-size`` (optional, default ``128``)
+
+``create-endpoint=off`` keeps launch policy fail-fast if the selected endpoint
+does not already exist in the host bridge backend.
+Use ``create-endpoint=on`` only when you explicitly want QEMU to create a
+missing endpoint (for example, offline staging workflows).
+
+In transport mode, the bridge is attached with ``vmm_max_msg_size=128``, and
+the default ring geometry is 256 entries by 128-byte slots for both TX and RX.
+For the current v1 bridge UAPI, transport-mode ring geometry is effectively
+fixed to that 256x128 shape rather than a general tuning knob.
+Endpoint ``max_msg_size`` is still learned per attach epoch from ``ATTACH``
+and later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` lifecycle refresh events, and may be smaller.
+For standalone mode, both rings must use exactly ``256`` entries, and
+``entry-size`` must equal the attach-time ``vmm-max-msg-size`` rounded up to
+the bridge alignment (``128`` bytes in the current UAPI).
+
+Operational Notes
+-----------------
+
+- Attach may succeed while the endpoint is offline.
+- ``ATTACH`` is the authoritative initial capability snapshot for one attach
+  epoch. It provides the transport revision and feature bits, plus endpoint
+  online/offline state and initial endpoint max message size when already
+  online.
+- If attach already reports the endpoint online, QEMU uses that snapshot
+  directly and does not wait for a synthetic ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` event.
+- ``RESOLVE_ENDPOINT`` uses create flags only when ``create-endpoint=on``.
+- Default policy is ``create-endpoint=off`` for explicit pre-provisioned
+  endpoints.
+- QEMU keeps control-path receive active while an attach is offline or waiting
+  for topology publication, but it blocks endpoint-scoped RX, TX, map-event,
+  and pending required-delivery flush work until topology publication
+  succeeds for the current online epoch.
+- QEMU publishes a full topology snapshot through ``SET_DEVICES`` after the
+  bridge is attached and machine initialization is complete when the attach
+  snapshot is already online, and republishes the full snapshot after each
+  later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` transition.
+- The published transport topology uses external device numbers
+  ``[0..VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_DEVS - 1]``.
+- ``SET_DEVICES`` uses inclusive ``first_dev_num`` and ``last_dev_num`` values,
+  and bitmap bit ``n`` maps to ``first_dev_num + n``.
+- Runtime child hotplug and unplug are unsupported.
+- The ``virtio-msg`` proxy bus is intentionally not hotpluggable.
+- While offline or still waiting for ``SET_DEVICES`` to complete for a new
+  online epoch, transmit requests return retry and required-delivery TX is not
+  queued.
+- Required traffic (synchronous responses plus used/config notifications) is
+  queued for eventual delivery when transient backpressure occurs, with a
+  bounded timer-backed retry (1..64 ms) only after ``SET_DEVICES`` succeeds
+  for the current online epoch.
+- Required traffic retry is canceled when the queue drains or the endpoint
+  leaves that active online state.
+- Generic DMA windows are published and revoked by bridge map events through
+  provider-backed RAM subregions in the bridge DMA address space.
+- DMA visibility comes from those bridge map events rather than from any
+  ``GET_SHM`` transport message path.
+- ``ADD`` map events may advertise ``mmap_length`` larger than the active DMA
+  window; QEMU maps the delivered extent and exposes exactly ``length`` bytes
+  of guest-visible DMA space.
+- ``DEL_REQ`` map events revoke DMA visibility immediately, return
+  ``MAP_ACK_RETRY`` while local references remain, and return
+  ``MAP_ACK_OK`` only on a later retry after local teardown completes.
+- QEMU does not evict installed mappings only to free cache space.
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is an attached-but-offline transition, not a detach.
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` clears pending required-delivery TX state, clears
+  pending map-ACK state, revokes all live bridge DMA mappings, and resets
+  attached child devices.
+- The child-device reset on ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is retained QEMU transport
+  policy, not a Linux bridge ABI requirement.
+- Guest queue accesses and later DMA buffers use the bridge-managed DMA
+  address space, so map-event updates directly control when published DMA
+  ranges become usable or removable.
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` and ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` are the only runtime endpoint
+  lifecycle notifications.
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` is a lifecycle refresh payload carrying ``endpoint_id``,
+  ``bus_name``, and endpoint ``max_msg_size`` only; it does not republish
+  revision or feature bitmaps.
+- ``dev_fd`` carries both endpoint control notifications and map events.
+- ``ATTACH`` validates revision and initial max message size.
+- Runtime ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` validates endpoint ``max_msg_size`` only, using
+  ``0 -> VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE`` with
+  ``virtio_msg_header_size() <= max_msg_size <= 128``.
+- Bridge map events accept ``bus_addr == 0`` as long as length and alignment
+  requirements are satisfied.
+
+Limitations
+-----------
+
+- Migration is blocked for this backend (bridge session state is host-local).
+
+Troubleshooting
+---------------
+
+- Confirm the bridge device node exists and permissions are correct.
+- Confirm the selected endpoint exists and is valid for the backend bus.
+- If the endpoint is offline, expect retry behavior until an online transition.
+- If transport devices do not appear after boot, confirm machine
+  initialization completed and the endpoint reached the online state so QEMU
+  could publish the topology snapshot through ``SET_DEVICES`` and move the
+  session into its active online state.
+- If online events are rejected, verify bridge revision and max message size
+  match the supported capability contract.
+
+Tracing
+-------
+
+QEMU tracepoints are available for the Linux bridge backend. To enable all
+bridge trace events:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -trace enable=virtio_msg_linux_bridge_*
+
+To discover event names:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -trace help
+
+and filter for ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_``.
+
+To write traces to a file with the ``simple`` trace backend:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   -trace enable=virtio_msg_linux_bridge_*,file=/tmp/vmsg.trace
+
+If QEMU is built without ``simple`` backend support, traces can still be
+captured by redirecting stderr.
+
+Developer Notes
+---------------
+
+Implementation details and internal contracts are documented in QEMU's
+developer documentation.
